🧠 What “LAZY” really means in Java Streams
A stream does NOTHING until a terminal operation is called.
And when it executes, it processes elements ONE BY ONE through the entire pipeline.

🔑 Two types of stream operations (this is the foundation)
1️⃣ Intermediate operations (LAZY)
Examples:
map
filter
sorted
distinct
limit
They do not run immediately.
They only build a pipeline.

2️⃣ Terminal operations (TRIGGER execution)
Examples:
forEach
collect
findFirst
anyMatch
count
reduce
👉 The moment a terminal operation is called, execution starts.

🧪 Let’s see this in action (VERY IMPORTANT)
Stream.of(1, 2, 3)
    .map(x -> {
        System.out.println("map " + x);
        return x * 2;
    })
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("filter " + x);
        return x > 2;
    });

❓ What prints?
👉 NOTHING
Why?
No terminal operation
Stream is never executed

🔥 Now add a terminal operation
Stream.of(1, 2, 3)
    .map(x -> {
        System.out.println("map " + x);
        return x * 2;
    })
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("filter " + x);
        return x > 2;
    })
    .forEach(x -> System.out.println("forEach " + x));

✅ Output (READ CAREFULLY)
map 1
filter 2
map 2
filter 4
forEach 4
map 3
filter 6
forEach 6

🧠 THIS is the MOST IMPORTANT realization
Streams do NOT work like loops over stages.
They work like loops over elements.

❌ WRONG mental model
map all → filter all → forEach all

✅ CORRECT mental model
take 1 → map → filter → forEach
take 2 → map → filter → forEach
take 3 → map → filter → forEach
This is called vertical execution.

🔑 Why Java made Streams lazy (this will calm you)
Reason 1️⃣ Performance
Stops early (findFirst, anyMatch)
Avoids unnecessary work

Reason 2️⃣ Memory efficiency
No intermediate collections
One element at a time

Reason 3️⃣ Composability00
You can build pipelines safely

🧠 Example that proves laziness (interview GOLD)
Stream.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("filter " + x);
        return x > 3;
    })
    .findFirst();

Output:
filter 1
filter 2
filter 3
filter 4

❗ Notice:
5 is never processed
Stream stops immediately after finding 4
This is laziness + short-circuiting.

🔑 Rule you must remember forever
Intermediate ops describe WHAT to do.
Terminal ops decide WHEN to do it.

🚫 Common Stream mistakes (you were probably doing this)
❌ Expecting intermediate ops to run
list.stream().map(x -> x * 2); // does nothing

❌ Debugging streams without peek
.peek(System.out::println)

🧭 How to THINK before writing a stream
Before writing a stream, ask:
1️⃣ What is my source?
2️⃣ What transformations do I want?
3️⃣ What is my terminal goal?
If you don’t know the terminal operation → stop.

🎯 One-sentence mental anchor (VERY IMPORTANT)
Streams are lazy pipelines that execute element-by-element only when a terminal operation demands a result.

