🔍 Loop vs Stream (execution comparison)
❌ Typical loop
for (int x : list) {
    if (x > 10) {
        result = x;
        break; // you must remember to break
    }
}

✅ Stream
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x > 10)
    .findFirst();

No manual break
Stops internally
Cleaner + safer

🔑 Rule to remember forever
Short-circuiting terminal operations (findFirst, anyMatch, limit)
can stop stream execution early, making them efficient.

When would a traditional for loop be better than a stream?
A traditional for loop is better when index-based access is required, when complex control flow is needed (break, continue, multiple conditions),
or when readability and step-by-step debugging are more important than functional style.

